\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Baht Ends Off 11-Wk Low; Medium-Term Bonds Lower  \b0\par\par\uc2 754 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 12:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht fell in Asian spot trade Monday to close just off an 11-week low after the dollar strengthened against global as well as regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and due to suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Medium-term government bond prices were lower, under pressure from rising Treasury bill yields. Dealers expect further flattening in the long end of the yield curve if a government bond auction Wednesday confirms that demand is still significantly higher than supply.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar finished at THB39.15, up from THB39.00 at the Asian close Friday, after rising to an 11-week high of THB39.18.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore players were believed to have actively chased the dollar up in the morning session to cover short positions, triggering stop-loss orders, a Bangkok-based trader said.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. unit was well bid as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected to have joined the buying spree," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was also helped by hefty cross-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand from Thailand-based Japanese companies, which bought the yen after purchasing the U.S. dollar with the baht to make payments on loans and repatriate earnings before the end of Japan's fiscal year on March 31.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's rally was exaggerated by thin volumes due to the closure of many European markets for Easter Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Exporters sold heavily around THB39.10 for month-end settlements, but the amount wasn't sufficient to hold back the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Traders gave a wide THB38.80-THB39.30 range for support and resistance overnight as the U.S. markets react to the sharp changes in the dollar's value later in the global day. [ 28-03-05 1046GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Medium-term government bond yields edged up in a thin market under pressure from rising treasury bill yields. Most long-term bond yields were, however, unchanged or edged lower, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 T-bill yields rose in line with auction results, which showed a drop in demand. Earlier in the day, the Bank of Thailand sold THB6 billion worth of 91-day T-bills at an average 2.095%, up from 2.061% at last week's auction. It sold another THB5 billion in 182-day T-bills at an average 2.278%, up from 2.259%.  \par \par \uc2 Funds set up recently to invest in short-term paper appeared to have completed their initial buying, and nonresident demand for T-bills may have weakened due to the baht's recent weakness against the dollar, a dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai yield curve is likely to flatten further if Wednesday's auction confirms that demand is still greater than supply in the long end, another dealer said. Investors will also be watching Tuesday's auction of short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds to be certain that it follows the trend set by T-bills.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai Bond Dealing Center said the average bid late Monday on the paper due December 2006 was virtually unchanged at 2.800%; the November 2009 bid edged up half a basis point to 3.806%; the August 2010 bid gained one basis point to 3.933%; the March 2012 bid was virtually unchanged at 4.185%; the May 2014 bid edged down half a basis point to 4.488%; the December 2014 bid was virtually unchanged at 4.601%; and the April 2021 bid was down half a basis point at 4.950%.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight interbank rate was quoted around 1.86%-1.89% in late trade, versus a most-active level Friday of 1.90%, dealers said. Liquidity was high as banks and clients prepared extra cash for month-end payments, but the overnight repurchase market rate came off a very low level late Friday of 1.75% to close at 1.78125%.  \par \par \uc2 Quotes for term interest rates were mixed. Dealers said the market was pausing because rates have climbing steadily in recent weeks, in line with the expected trend in official interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Market     Term            Monday                Friday \par Interbank  Overnight/ \par            Most-Active     1.86%-1.9% (Estimate) 1.90000% \par Repurchase Overnight/Close 1.78125% (Actual)     1.75000% \par            14-Day/Close    2.25000%              2.25000% \par Bibor      3-Month/11 am   2.63732%              2.63766% \par            6-Month/11 am   2.83661%              2.83453% \par THB Fix    6-Month/11 am   3.07091%              3.07277% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Fox and Maytinee Wasumadee, Dow Jones Newswires; 662 266 0744; djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -Edited by Ven Ram \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 28-03-05 1058GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1028 1861  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050328e13s0009a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
