\par\par \b \uc2 MOODY'S ASSIGNS (P)Ba1 RATING TO THAI MILITARY BANK'S JUNIOR SUBORDINATED NOTES  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,060 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par \uc2 Moody's Investors Service Press Release\par \uc2 MOODPR\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005  \par \par \uc2 Moody's Investor's Service has assigned a rating of (P)Ba1 to Thai Military Bank's (TMB's) proposed junior subordinated debt notes, due 2015. The rating reflects TMB's systemic importance, its improving, but still weak financial fundamentals, as well as the structure of this subordinated issue. The ratings outlook is stable.  \par \par \uc2 Note that this rating is subject to the receipt of final documentation, the terms and conditions of which have not changed in any material way from the draft documents reviewed by Moody's.  \par \par \uc2 As with many Upper Tier II capital-qualifying subordinated debt issues, TMB's junior subordinated securities include provisions that allow the borrower to defer payment of interest, under certain circumstances and prohibiting the payment of principal and interest under others. If implemented, such provisions (while not legally constituting an event of default) could result in payment terms appreciably different from senior securities. These distinctions are reflected in the two notch rating differential between TMB's Baa2 rating on senior obligations and this Ba1 rated junior subordinated debt issue.  \par \par \uc2 While, Moody's considers TMB to possess sufficient financial flexibility (supplemented as it is by regulatory support) to avoid, in most circumstances, deferring interest payments on its junior subordinated debt, the solvency conditions required by Thailand's regulators in Upper Tier II capital-qualifying debt are particularly strict.  \par \par \uc2 Specifically, the bond indenture specifies that in addition to the usual solvency requirement, "No principal or interest shall be payable in respect of the Notes ... if the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by ordering a capital write down and increase."  \par \par \uc2 While Moody's expects regulatory support will ultimately benefit all creditor classes, this provision could affect the timeliness of such benefit reaching junior subordinated creditors. Theoretically, non-payment after an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could persist until such time as common dividends are reinstated. Having said that, Moody's pointed out that if current positive economic and bank performance trends persist, it will become progressively more unlikely that TMB would require such assistance.  \par \par \uc2 Thai Military Bank, headquartered in Bangkok, is Thailand's fifth largest bank, with over 450 branches countrywide. It had total assets of THB 669 billion (US$ 17 billion) on December 31, 2004. The Thai military's aggregate stake in TMB has declined significantly since the Asian financial crisis of 1997 and now stands at only 5.4%. The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the largest shareholder with 31.2%. Singapore's DBS Bank holds 16.1%.  \par \par \uc2 Thai Military Bank's other ratings are:  \par \par \uc2 Long and short-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits of Baa2 and Prime-2, respectively; Long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  subordinated debt of Baa3; and Bank Financial Strength (BFSR) of E+. The BFSR has a positive outlook, while all debt and deposit ratings carry a stable outlook.  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Moody's Investors Service, Inc. and/or its licensors including Moody's Assurance Company, Inc. (together, "MOODY'S"). All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 ALL INFORMATION CONTAINED HEREIN IS PROTECTED BY COPYRIGHT LAW AND NONE OF SUCH INFORMATION MAY BE COPIED OR OTHERWISE REPRODUCED, REPACKAGE