\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Baht Ends Up On Equities Inflows, Yen; Bonds Higher  \b0\par\par\uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 11:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht ended marginally higher against the dollar in Asian spot trade Tuesday, boosted by equities-related investment inflows and the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mild weakness against the Japanese yen, a regional trendsetter, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's losses were limited, however, due to suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices closed higher on demand driven by high cash liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar finished at THB38.34, down from THB38.39 at Monday's close.  \par \par \uc2 Following overnight weakness related to its weak tone against the yen, the dollar was sold off in early trade from the THB38.33 level by offshore players from Singapore. The selling, which was probably related to equities investments, forced the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to an intraday low of THB38.30, a Bangkok-based trader said.  \par \par \uc2 The selling was met by strong support from suspected agents of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar was well bid at the THB38.31 level with heavy buying orders placed by some agents," the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  made a late recovery after failing to break under support, but the gains were limited by its generally weak or directionless tone against the yen and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the dollar to remain in a range of THB38.30 to THB38.40 overnight. [ 08-03-05 1042GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Most Thai bond yields fell in anticipation of strong demand in Wednesday's government bond auction, and after high cash liquidity sharply limited the rise of yields in Tuesday's auction of short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai Bond Dealing Center said the average bid late Tuesday on the government bond due December 2006 fell two basis points to 2.867%, the December 2008 bid dropped one basis point to 3.518%, the November 2009 bid was off marginally at 3.919%, the August 2010 bid was down one basis point to 4.071%, and the December 2014 bid eased almost half a basis point to 4.710%, as did the December 2019 bid to 5.025%.  \par \par \uc2 Yields in Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond auction rose, but not as much as expected after last week's interest rate increase, due to demand driven by high cash levels of both banks and popular short-term funds. Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its 14-day repurchase market target rate by 25 basis points to 2.25%.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Thailand sold at an auction Tuesday THB5 billion worth of 28-day \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds at an average 1.883%, up from 1.853% at the previous auction. A THB3 billion tranche of 63-day \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds was sold at an average 1.968%, up from 1.967%. Another THB10 billion of 364-day \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds was sold at an average 2.568%, up from 2.552% last week.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight interbank rate was quoted late Tuesday around 1.85%, after early trade in a range of 1.95%-2%, versus the most-active level of 1.875% and a range of 1.7%-2.1% Monday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The most-active level was expected to rise with the start of a new reserve period Tuesday, as cash is freed up to move to higher-yielding markets.  \par \par \uc2 It may not have risen, however, because liquidity remained very high, as reflected in the 28-day accepted yields and the overnight repurchase market rate, which closed flat at 1.75%.  \par \par \uc2 Quotes for most term interest rates edged higher again, as increases in onshore and offshore interest rates are expected to continue in the coming months.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Market     Term            Tuesday              Monday \par Interbank  Overnight/ \par            Most-Active     1.85%-1.95%(Estimate)1.87500% \par Repurchase Overnight/Close 1.75000% (Actual)    1.75000% \par            14-Day/Close    2.25000%             2.25000% \par Bibor      3-Month/11 am   2.61875%             2.61094% \par            6-Month/11 am   2.79391%             2.79031% \par THB Fix    6-Month/11 am   3.02002%             3.02720% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Fox and Maytinee Wasumadee, Dow Jones Newswires; 66 2266 0744; djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hilary Mc Cully [ 08-03-05 1054GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1028 1861  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050308e138000le\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
