\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Baht Ends Up; Ctrl Bk Suspected To Have Capped Rise  \b0\par\par\uc2 542 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2004\par 10:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht ended stronger in Asian spot trade Thursday amid general dollar weakness, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was believed to have stepped in to help cap the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at 38.865 baht, down from THB38.965 at the Asian close Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell against most Asian and global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan said late Wednesday that U.S. interest rates are likely to remain at current lows for an extended period.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Thailand Assistant Governor Tasna Rajatabhothi said Thursday that Greenspan's comments helped to trigger the baht's rise, but that so far its movements against the dollar were in line with those of other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is monitoring the baht's movements closely, she said, indirectly reaffirming a long-standing policy of buying dollars on weakness to prevent excessive volatility and build foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 A Bangkok-based dealer said it appeared that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying provided strong support for the dollar Thursday at THB38.85. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  normally doesn't comment on day-to-day trading \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to trade overnight in a range of THB38.80 to THB38.90, he said, but a test and a successful break of January's 42-month low of THB38.80 was likely by next week.  \par \par \uc2 Economists expect the baht to continue appreciating due to both the dollar's weakness and to strong inflows to Thailand for investment and through the current account.  \par \par \uc2 Another Bangkok-based dealer said the dollar was likely to be dropping at a very slow rate of about THB0.05 a day, due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and central banks across Asia.  \par \par \uc2 Thai government bond prices rose Thursday on Greenspan's indication that U.S. interest rates would remain low for an extended period, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Gains were limited, however, as prices had already risen because of uncertainty regarding the effects of the bird flu outbreak on the local economy, they said.  \par \par \uc2 Short-term securities were mostly unchanged, as the yield curve is already very steep.  \par \par \uc2 The bid yield of the government bond due October 2005 ended virtually flat at 1.40%, while the April 2010 bid was off three basis points at 3.05%, and the March 2014 bid was down two basis points at 4.64%, the Thai Bond Dealing Center said.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers expected trade to remain range-bound ahead of the weekend, and pending further developments in the local bird flu situation or any sharp movements in U.S. Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight interest rates were flat Thursday, supported against the weight of excess financial system liquidity by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's defense of its 1.25% 14-day repurchase market target rate, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Market     Term            Thursday          Wednesday \par Interbank  Overnight/Avg   1.03000%          1.03000% \par Repurchase Overnight/Close 0.96875%          0.96875% \par            14-Day          1.25000%          1