\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Baht Ends Up On General Dlr Weakness;Resistance Firm  \b0\par\par\uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2004\par 11:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht ended slightly stronger in Asian spot trade Thursday amid widespread dollar weakness in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but gains were limited due to fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a possible avian flu outbreak, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar closed at 39.035 baht, down from THB39.06 at Wednesday's Asian close.  \par \par \uc2 Volume was thin with many local players sidelined for the Lunar New Year, although it isn't an official holiday in Thailand.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said that despite broad dollar weakness, there was no threat to support at THB39 due to expectations the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at that level and on reports that the regional outbreak of avian flu may have spread to Thailand.  \par \par \uc2 A Japanese Agriculture Ministry official in Tokyo said the country temporarily suspended chicken meat imports from Thailand Thursday following reports that several people are being tested for the disease.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai government insisted that there have been no confirmed cases yet, and that widespread culling of poultry in the country has been to contain outbreaks of avian cholera and bronchitis.  \par \par \uc2 Thailand is among the world's top five chicken exporters, and last year shipped 540,000 metric tons of chicken valued at THB50 billion, according to the Thai Broilers Processing Exporters Association.  \par \par \uc2 It had targeted exports of 630,000 metric tons worth THB70 billion this year.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers expect the dollar to remain in a tight THB39.00-THB39.10 range Friday, with avian flu fears continuing to assist the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in resisting the tendency for the baht to appreciate on current account and investment flows.  \par \par \uc2 Prices of Thai government bonds ended mostly higher Thursday on fears that the avian flu outbreak could hurt related economic activity and reduce the attractiveness of stocks as an alternative investment, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The bourse's SET Index closed Thursday down 0.9% at 760.17 points. The agricultural sector index was off 4% at 56.79 points.  \par \par \uc2 Gains in bonds were limited, however, as economic growth and indicators remained strong, the avian flu outbreak was unconfirmed and long-term yields were considered likely to rise this year regardless.  \par \par \uc2 The bid-yield on the issue due October 2005 ended Thursday down one basis point at 1.357% and the bid on the issue due February 2008 was off four basis points at 2.334%, the Thai Bond Dealing Center said.  \par \par \uc2 The March 2014 bid dropped four basis points to 4.979%, but a last deal of 4.90% indicated that bidders were having to hit offers and that yields might be falling a little further Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Volume remained thin with many investors sidelined for the Lunar New Year holiday.  \par \par \uc2 The Finance Ministry auctioned government-guaranteed state enterprise bonds Thursday at slight premiums to the government bond yield curve, but within expectations.  \par \par \uc2 Six-year Expressway & Rapid Transit Authority bonds were sold at an accepted yield of 3.40%, three-year Government Housing Bank bonds at 1.85%, and five-year GHB bonds at 2.965%.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight interest rates were unchanged, supported by banks' preparations for extra cash demand over the holiday and the end Thursday of a biweekly reserve period and capped by excess financial system liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Market     Term      