\par\par \b \uc2 PRESS DIGEST - Thai newspapers January 14.  \b0\par\par\uc2 321 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2004\par 02:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, Jan 14 (Reuters) - These are some of the leading stories in the Thai press on Wednesday. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy.  \par \par \uc2 TOP STORIES  \par \par \uc2 - Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra has blamed officials in the south and their "accumulated weaknesses" - a culture of work in which state agencies lacked the will to forge ties among themselves and with local people - for the resurgent violence in three Muslim-dominated provinces (BANGKOK POST)  \par \par \uc2 - The government will appeal, by the end of January, the civil court ruling it must pay six billion baht ($153 million) compensation to the Bang Na-Chon Buri expressway contractor - joint venture BBCD - which is 35-percent owned by Ch Karnchang (CK.BK) and the rest by two German companies, Bilfinger and Berger Bauaktiengesellschaft (BANGKOK POST)  \par \par \uc2 BUSINESS  \par \par \uc2 - Thailand's third largest bank, Kasikornbank PCL (KBAN.BK), announced a restructuring of its top management ranks with the appointment of Prasarn Trairatvorakul, who stepped down as secretary-general of the Securities and Exchange Commission in December, as president (BANGKOK POST)  \par \par \uc2 - Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra says the government is willing to spend up to 20 billion baht ($513 million) to stabilise the price of fuel at local pumps (BANGKOK POST)  \par \par \uc2 - To keep economic growth on target, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to alleviate pressure that is pushing the baht higher, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said (BANGKOK POST)  \par \par \uc2 - Advertising agency SC Matchbox, a subsidiary of Shin Corp (SHIN.BK), will focus less on winning new accounts from government agencies (THE NATION)  \par \par \uc2 - Charoen Pokphand group plans to invest billions of baht to expand its business empire in China, with the focus on three core business areas - food, retail and bio-technology (THE NATION)  \par \par \uc2 ($1=38.98 Baht)  \par \par \uc2 ** Looking for more information from local sources? Factiva.com has eight Thai sources including the Bangkok Post and Business Day.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040114e01e0006a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
