\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Baht Ends Up On Moody's; Off 39-Month Intraday High  \b0\par\par\uc2 696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2003\par 12:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht strengthened further in Asia Friday, hitting a fresh 39-month intraday high, as players continued to sell dollars following news Thursday that Moody's Investor Service may upgrade Thailand's sovereign credit rating.  \par \par \uc2 However, the dollar was pulled off its lows on concerns of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the late recovery in the dollar/yen. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a regional trendsetter.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar closed at 39.51 baht, off an intraday low of THB39.49, and down from THB39.65 at the end of the Asian spot session Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar opened at an intraday high of THB39.58 but gradually lost ground on the prospect of an upgrade.  \par \par \uc2 "The Moody's revision continued to weigh on dollar sentiment. The news seemed to have made the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increasingly reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now, as it doesn't want the baht to resist its bullish trend," said a Bangkok-based trader.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Thailand Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said in the afternoon that the baht's appreciation was due to the possibility of a ratings upgrade by Moody's and Thailand's strong economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 He also said there were no signs of speculation.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it will only act to prevent speculative, exaggerated moves in the dollar/baht.  \par \par \uc2 After Pridiyathorn's comments the dollar hit an intraday low of THB39.49 but later regained some strength on its gains against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Late in Asia, the dollar was quoted at Y110.43, up slightly from a low of Y110.22.  \par \par \uc2 "Despite Pridiyathorn's remark, some players still were concerned the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step in because it isn't likely to allow the greenback to give up the key THB39.50 easily," another trader said.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the dollar to find support at THB39.30 Monday and be capped on the upside at THB39.70, subject to volatility in the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Prices of Thai government bonds ended sharply lower Friday due to rising concerns about upcoming supply of various government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt securities, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 A local newspaper reported Friday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to issue THB30 billion in seven-year savings bonds to institutional investors in early December. The issue is to be part of a planned THB200 billion of various types of government bonds to be offered in the fiscal year that began Oct. 1 to help refinance the debt of the state Financial Institutions Development Fund.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has informed local banks that it plans to sell THB50 billion of three-month promissory notes soon, probably next week.  \par \par \uc2 The news came on top of issues announced earlier this week of THB30 billion worth of regular government bonds in the current quarter, and THB42 billion in treasury bills in October.  \par \par \uc2 "There is lots of supply coming to the market, while demand has dried up due to a shift of funds to the more active stock market," a local bank dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in during the morning session to buy medium and long-term bonds in a bid to calm the market, but the losses continued, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The rise in yields was especially apparent in long-term bonds, but bid-offer spreads remained wide and movement was exaggerated in thin volume, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The government bond due November 2011 closed in a bid-offer range of 3.25%-3.35%, up from a previous close of 3.20%, they said. The issue due December 2008 ended at 2.23%-2.20% versus 2.13%.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight interest rates ended flat, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to borrow as needed to support rates against the weight of excess financial system liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Market     Term            Friday            Thursday \par Interbank  Overnight/Late  1.10000%          1.10000% \par Repurchase Overnight/Close 1.09375%          1.09375% \par            14-Day          1.25000%          1.25000% \par            1-Month         no activity       1.25000% \par            3-Month         no activity       no activity \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Rattaphol Onsanit and Suttinee Yuvejwattana, Dow Jones Newswires; 662 266 0744; djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Simon Harvey  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1028 1861  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031003dza3000fp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
