\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Baht Ends Steady; Early Fall Recouped On Higher Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2003\par 12:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht ended steady against the dollar Tuesday, pulled off its early lows on the yen's gains, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra's bullish comments on the baht also weighed on sentiment toward the dollar, they said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ended at 39.985 baht, compared with THB39.98 at the Asian close Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar short-covering by offshore players in the morning helped the dollar to claw up to an intraday high of THB40.14 from its 38-month low Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders also suspected the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the baht's appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 However, the dollar resumed its downward course as market players sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it slipped against the yen, the regional trendsetter.  \par \par \uc2 "Despite the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 , players still based their decision on the dollar's recent general weakness," said a trader.  \par \par \uc2 Late in Asia the dollar was at Y111.15 against the yen compared with Y120 early in the session and around Y111 late in Asia Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has come under immense pressure since the Group of Seven finance ministers called for exchange rate flexibility over the weekend.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar went down to its intraday low of THB39.95 after Thaksin said the baht could rise further, if it's supported by economic fundamentals, adding that the baht's recent appreciation was in line with the regional trend.  \par \par \uc2 In a sign of support for the G7 statement, Thaksin also agreed that governments and central banks shouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and should let the exchange rate move "naturally" as long as it wasn't too volatile.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers expect the dollar to open in Asia Wednesday in a range of THB39.80 to THB40.20, subject to volatility in the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Prices of long-term Thai government bonds ended lower Tuesday, tracking losses overnight in U.S. Treasurys, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Losses in the issue due November 2011 were exaggerated, however, by positioning in thin trade ahead of an auction reopening the issue Wednesday, a dealer at a foreign bank said.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the November 2011 issue ended at 3.18%, up from 3.09% at Monday's close, the Thai Bond Dealing Center said in its daily report.  \par \par \uc2 "The November 2011 yield is well above the curve. Some players seemed to be pushing it up so they can buy the bond cheaply at tomorrow's (Wednesday's) auction," the dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 The yield on the government bond due March 2011 finished more in line with the yield curve at 2.92%, compared with an average bid/offer range of around 2.88%/2.835% in active trade Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Short-term issues closed unchanged or down only slightly, as they received continued support from demand driven by high financial system liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 The government bond due December 2006 closed at 1.525%, up just a tad from a previous close of 1.52%.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers expect the market to trade in a fairly tight range pending details of the government's issuance plans for the year beginning Oct. 1.  \par \par \uc2 An auction Tuesday of THB3 billion worth of 364-day \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds resulted in a weighted-average accepted yield of around 1.218%, up from 1.206% last week.  \par \par \uc2 Accepted yields were a little higher than secondary market levels, reflecting uncertainty over the direction of yields pending information on upcoming supply and tightness in overnight liquidity. The secondary market was unaffected, however, as the tightness was only temporary and the uncertainty has largely been factored in to prices.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight interest rates fell back to normal levels by the close Tuesday, after rising temporarily on demand for baht associated with dollar weakness at the end of a biweekly cash reserve period for local banks on Monday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Market     Term            Tuesday           Monday \par Interbank  Overnight/Late  1.10000%          1.15000% \par Repurchase Overnight/Close 1.09375%          1.12500% \par            14-Day          1.25000%          1.25000% \par            1-Month         no activity       1.25000% \par            3-Month         no activity       no activity \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Rattaphol Onsanit and Tom Fox, Dow Jones Newswires; 662 266 0744; djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Simon Harvey  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1028 1861  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030923dz9n000h3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
