\par\par \b \uc2  Thai PM: APEC Mins Discuss Need For Floating  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 310 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2003\par 06:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  PHUKET, Thailand -(Dow Jones)- Thai Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra said Thursday that Asia Pacific Economic Cooperation finance ministers talks centered on the need to promote free-float exchange rate regimes in the region, but there was no mention of China's rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. \par \par \uc2  "We talked about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' flexibility but not the yuan in particular," Thaksin said. \par \par \uc2  He said he didn't expect China to float the yuan any time soon but noted such a move would benefit the Thai economy's competitiveness in the region. \par \par \uc2  "They (China) will have to look for the right timing" for freeing their exchange rate, he said. \par \par \uc2  China is resisting pressure from the U.S. and Japan to loosen its grip on the yuan, which is tightly pegged to the dollar. U.S. manufacturers, who are losing their markets to cheap Chinese goods, argue the strict exchange rate regime keeps the yuan undervalued and have been pushing for a revaluation. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said Thursday upon his arrival here that a brighter outlook for economic growth and relatively tranquil markets make now a good time for Asian governments to wean themselves from reliance on capital controls and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But he acknowledged his message will be a tough sell. Although much attention has been paid to China's allegedly undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , several important trading nations in the region \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to prevent their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating and making exports less competitive. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem the baht's appreciation against the dollar, banned the lending of securities offshore in a bid to restrict the inflow of speculative funds that were driving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. \par \par \uc2  -By Suttinee Yuvejwattana, Dow Jones Newswires; 661-8677635 \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Mary de Wet \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030904dz9400088\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
