\par\par \b \uc2  Thai  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 , bond markets jolted by easing. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Vithoon Amorn \par \uc2 523 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2003\par 10:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BANGKOK, Aug 21 (Reuters) - Thailand's decision to let its fund managers invest more in offshore debt instruments has jolted the bond market, pushing up yields and forcing companies to rethink borrowering plans. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Thailand (BOT) raised, to $2.45 billion from $500 million, the combined amount of offshore debt paper Thai funds and institutions can buy. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Wednesday's announcement was designed to ease upward pressure on the baht, which has risen about 3.6 percent against the dollar so far this year. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at Barclays Capital Securities (\b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) said the BOT announcement reflected a desire to make \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  less necessary to slow the baht's rallies. \par \par \uc2  "It seems too focused on achieving its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  objective without duly considering the impact on bond and capital markets," said the dealer who did not want to be identified. \par \par \uc2  The dealer said a key uncertainty was whether the BOT would continue to raise the offshore investment limit much further from here if requested to do so by fund managers. \par \par \uc2  Bond dealers said the BOT's move sent yields of medium and long-term government bonds up 10 to 20 basis points on Wednesday. Some expect further yield curve steepening in the next few days. \par \par \uc2  "It hits long-term yields. When the bank announced the original $500 million ceiling in July, yields shot up for three days. This sharply higher amount should push up yields for a few days," said Pannarat Bhanpato of MFC Asset Management. \par \par \uc2  The yield of seven-year government bond  was 2.33 percent on Thursday, up 16 basis points from Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  VOLATILITY \par \par \uc2  The BOT said it approved the increase because it had underestimated demand for investment in offshore debt securities. It said the higher outflow was not expected to affect domestic economic stability. \par \par \uc2  Market volatility and higher yields after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision prompted Thailand's largest finance firm, National Finance PCL (NFS.BK), to delay a planned four-billion-baht ($96.1 million) seven-year bond issue indefinitely. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said at least some of the 10 other Thai companies, planning to issue a total of about 45 billion baht of bonds in August and September, would probably also hold off. \par \par \uc2  Those which might be affected include a three billion baht issue of six-year bonds by Quality Houses PCL (QH.BK), a four-billion-baht, five-year secured amortising bond issue by Sahaviriya Steel Industries PCL (SSI.BK) and an issue of up to nine billion baht of secured, amortising bonds by mobile phone operator Total Access Communication PCL (TACC.SI). \par \par \uc2  "The market sell-off, higher yields and a general shift to stocks from bonds make it harder for pending issuers to go ahead with their plans. They are seeing the chance of locking low long-term coupons slip away," a fund manager said. \par \par \uc2  Analyst Saichon Fuvivattanakankun at the Bangkok Bond Dealers Centre said recent steepening of Thai long bond yield curve had trimmed average daily bond market turnover to eight billion baht this week from 12 billion baht in July. ($1 = 41.62 baht). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030821dz8l00hgb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
