\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Strengthen on the coat-tails of yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2003\par 06:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Ovais Subhani\par     SINGAPORE, Aug 8 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strengthened\par across the board on Friday, as the Japanese yen's rebound\par against the U.S. dollar prompted players to buy back regionals\par they had sold earlier in the week.\par     The yen rose to around 119 per dollar, from recent lows\par past 120, and the rise in Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was led by those\par that had been sold heavily this week.\par     "The tone is slightly better from the dollar/yen for all of\par the Asian regionals. That's why we are a little bit stronger,"\par said James Malcolm, strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Singapore.\par     The Indonesian rupiah        rose nearly half a percent\par from late Thursday to 8,550 per dollar. Earlier this week it\par tumbled to near three-month lows on the weaker side of 8,700\par per dollar, after a hotel in central Jakarta was bombed on\par Tuesday.\par     The Singapore dollar       , one of the region's most\par liquid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plays, strengthened to 1.7530, its highest\par since late July.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  It had fallen to a three-month low of 1.7668\par per dollar after the Jakarta bomb attack,.\par     Even the Philippine peso       , dogged by worries of\par political instability since a failed mutiny on July 27, managed\par to strengthen. The peso was up at around 54.60 per dollar, from\par Thursday's local close of 54.675 per dollar.\par     Malcolm said little has changed, however, in terms of the\par underlying sentiment mainly on the rupiah and peso --\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that would continue to be haunted by political woes.\par     Dealers in Jakarta said the rupiah could gain a bit more in\par the short-term if it manages to find support bat 8,550 per\par dollar.\par  \par     Dealers in Manila said the peso's rise might be limited by\par corporate dollar demand spotted around 54.60.\par     Malcolm said Asian central banks, sensitive to export\par competitiveness, would also dampen any significant gains.\par     "Given the deeper move in the dollar/yen, they may stay\par away in the very near term but further out they may come back,"\par he said.\par     Those concerns were true for South Korea, Taiwan and\par Thailand, where central banks have been active to head off\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains.\par     The yen's rise helped push the Korean won\par to its strongest since Monday, with more impetus added to the\par move by equity-related foreign inflows.\par     Still, fear of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was expected to keep the won's\par upside limited to 1,180 per dollar, dealers in Singapore said.\par     The Taiwan dollar          rose to a four-session high, but\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dealers expected a cap at 34.35-34.40. Recent net sales of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par equities by foreign investors was also likely to limit any\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par significant rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Thai baht  advanced to 41.87 per dollar, from\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par late Thursday's 41.93, aided by moves in the Sing dollar and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A strong domestic economy and sustained foreign equity\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par inflows have boosted the baht in recent months, but the gains\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par have been tempered by repeated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (BOT) Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par on Thursday there was no need for the bank to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par its recent easing of some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  restrictions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Malcolm said the comments were unlikely to alleviate market\par wariness over the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's tight \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  management.\par     "It seems likely that the BOT will remain active in the\par market until other central banks in the region begin to step\par aside from buying dollars to keep their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weak."\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR AT 0255 GMT\par  Change on the day\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move\par  Japan yen     119.14       120.13         +0.83\par  Sing dlr      1.7533       1.7582         +0.28\par  Taiwan dlr    34.417       34.446         +0.08\par  Korean won   1182.20      1184.00         +0.15\par  Baht           41.85        41.93         +0.19\par  Peso           54.62        54.68         +0.10\par  Rupiah       8550.00      8587.00         +0.43\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     119.14       118.74         -0.34\par  Sing dlr      1.7533       1.7341         -1.10\par  Taiwan dlr    34.417       34.753         +0.98\par  Korean won   1182.20      1185.80         +0.30\par