\par\par \b \uc2  Thai Baht Ends Up A Tad; Caution Ahead OF FOMC Meeting \b0\par\par\uc2 584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2003\par 11:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK -(Dow Jones)- The Thai baht ended marginally higher against the U.S. dollar Tuesday, boosted by the strength of the Singapore dollar amid position-adjustment ahead of an expected cut in U.S. interest rates this week, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar finished at 41.53 baht, down slightly from THB41.57 at the Asian close Monday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. unit has been trapped between THB41.45 and THB41.90 this month, with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying of dollars on weakness countering equities-related inflows and strong exports. \par \par \uc2  The dollar-baht exchange rate pressed down near the THB41.50 level in late trade Tuesday, after the U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar pushed lower, but investors remained wary of Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of heavy dollar buying below THB41.50 on several occasions recently. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose $500 million in the week to June 13. \par \par \uc2  Investors were also wary of short-term volatility after the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee announced its rate decision Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is expected to remain in THB41.50 to THB41.65 range Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Thai government bonds ended flat or slightly higher Tuesday, with the market mostly quiet ahead of the expected cut in U.S. interest rates, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Short- and medium-term securities fared slightly better than long-term paper due to buying to ease the burden of excess financial system liquidity and as a safe haven ahead of possible volatility after the expected cuts. \par \par \uc2  An auction of THB5 billion 364-day \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds Tuesday resulted in an average yield of around 1.54%, about one basis point below Monday's secondary market yield curve as expected. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the government bond due December 2006 ended in a bid-offer range of 1.71%-1.69%, down from a close of 1.72% Monday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The long-term issue due November 2012 finished at 2.53%-2.51%, flat from a previous close of 2.525%. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a 50-basis-point cut late Wednesday in the U.S. federal funds rate should lead to buying in Thai long-term bonds, as it would leave the market virtually certain of a cut of at least 25 basis points in the Thai 14-day repurchase market target rate, currently 1.75%. Bank of Thailand's monetary policy committee meets next on July 21. \par \par \uc2  Investors still expected a 25-basis-point cut in U.S. rates to lead to a matching cut in the Thai 14-day rate, but a smaller U.S. cut might leave them waiting for a look at July government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  auction schedules before deciding how aggressive they should be. The schedules should be issued late this week or early next week. \par \par \uc2  Overnight interbank rates fell to the lower end of their recent trading ranges, due to quiet trade ahead of the U.S. meeting on interest rates, but remained supported there by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowing to absorb excess liquidity in the system and defend the 14-day rate, dealers said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market     Term            Tuesday         Monday\par Interbank  Overnight/Late  1.65625%        1.65625%-1.6875%\par Repurchase Overnight/Close 1.65625%        1.65625%\par            14-Day          1.75000%        1.75000%\par            1-Month         no activity     no activity\par           