\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late, Mkt Waits For FOMC Decision \b0\par\par\uc2 1,022 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 2003\par 11:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded within tight ranges late Monday amid a general reluctance to take strong positions either way ahead of the U.S. Federal Reserve's decision on interest rates later this week. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, Philippine peso and Indonesian rupiah were modestly weaker. The South Korean won and New Taiwan dollar were marginally stronger, and the Thai baht was little changed. \par \par \uc2  While the Federal Open Market Committee is widely expected to cut rates Wednesday, the market is divided as to whether it would be a 25-basis-point or 50-basis-point reduction. \par \par \uc2  An aggressive cut would "conjure optimism about an early recovery in Asia, and perhaps inspire those who would seek to be long SGD (Singapore dollar), and inspire confidence in THB (Thai baht), which is enjoying a generous welter of portfolio inflows," Bank of America said in a note. \par \par \uc2  It might also excite yield seekers anew over the high-yielding Indonesian rupiah and Philippine peso, the bank added. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, U.S. dollar buying, mainly by local banks, kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buoyed against the Singapore unit. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a European bank said a drop in the city state's consumer price index for May stoked fears of deflation and kept the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a bid-ish tone against the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's consumer prices fell 0.1% on year in May, and was down 0.6% on month, the Department of Statistics said earlier Monday. \par \par \uc2  Near the close of local trading hours, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.7395, up from S$1.7360 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The European bank dealer said an increasing number of market players have been focusing on the Singapore dollar/Thai baht cross in recent weeks, fueled by expectations of a better outlook for the Thai economy compared to Singapore. \par \par \uc2  To that end, they have been selling the U.S. dollar against the baht and buying the U.S. dollar versus the Singapore unit. \par \par \uc2  As a result, the Singapore dollar/Thai baht cross has drifted to around 23.90 Monday from 24.20 about three weeks ago. This cross rate hardly moves, so such a shift may be considered significant, the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted around THB41.59 late in Asia, almost flat from THB41.60 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the session, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Pridiyathorn Devakula said there's no need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market now as the baht's level is satisfactory. \par \par \uc2  "We don't need to be involved (in the market) that much now. Let the baht move naturally," Pridiyathorn told reporters. He said equity-related inflows have strengthened the baht significantly. \par \par \uc2  J.P. Morgan Chase Bank said Thailand's latest foreign reserves data suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been actively buying dollars. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Thailand said Friday that its foreign reserves stood at $39.9 billion at June 13, up from $39.4 billion a week earlier. \par \par \uc2  J.P. Morgan Chase said that given the strong momentum in foreign buying of Thai shares, it remained positive on the baht and targets the dollar to fall gradually to THB41 by the end of the third quarter of 2003. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the South Korean won was supported by export settlements amid ample dollar supply, although concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the dollar from falling too much, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 1,190.2 won, a tad below Friday's KRW1,190.6. \par \par \uc2  Korean authorities weren't seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the U.S. unit following some aggressive moves last week, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The government did not have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market as concerns about such action alone prevented the dollar from retreating too far," said a dealer at a local bank in Seoul. \par \par \uc2  Most players expect the authorities to step in if the U.S. dollar moves below KRW1,190, particularly if such a decline is perceived as being too rapid. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was on a slightly weaker footing on dollar demand from local importers ahead of the month-end, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed the local session at 8,250 rupiah, slightly up from IDR8,240 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rise was capped by expectations of further capital inflows in the wake of Bank Mandiri's initial public offering of shares, which has attracted robust foreign demand. \par \par \uc2  The government has said it will raise the IPO size to four billion shares from three billion. The government is expected to raise around $300 million from the offering, the largest by a local company since the 1997-98 Asian financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  "Good news on Bank Mandiri helped curbed the dollar's rise," said a dealer with a European bank in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso snapped a three-day winning streak to close lower on corporate demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at the intraday high of 53.400 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP53.300 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Several banks also bought back some of the dollars they sold in previous sessions amid an ongoing debate on how much the Federal Reserve will likely cut U.S. rates. \par \par \uc2  Remarks by Finance Secretary Jose Isidro Camacho that the government doesn't have an immediate plan to tap the international debt market didn't have an impact on the peso. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to have stayed on the sidelines throughout the session. A senior \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official denied the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  is buying dollars now, so it can sell them later to enhance the peso, in time for next month's State of Nation Address of President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar was supported by foreign buying of local shares. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. ended at NT$34.591, down a tad from Friday's NT$34.593. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors bought a net NT$1.68 billion worth of shares on the Taiwan bourse Monday, according to the Taiwan Stock Exchange. \par \par \uc2  -By Nirmala Menon, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4046; nirmala.menon@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030623dz6n000mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
