\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Bounce back, upside seen limited. \b0\par\par\uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2003\par 04:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Ovais Subhani\par     SINGAPORE, June 20 (Reuters) - Most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par bounced in early Friday trade off lows hit in the previous\par session as players priced in a softer tone in the U.S. dollar\par against the yen .\par     The Singapore dollar        and Thai baht        edged up\par from one-week lows and the South Korean won\par recovered from its lowest level in about two weeks.\par     Dealers said despite the supportive yen move and equity\par investment flows, players were reluctant to take aggressive\par positions before the weekend and ahead of the U.S. Federal\par Reserve policy meeting next week.\par     In Singapore, the mood was likely to remain cautious ahead\par of the country's own policy announcement sometime in early\par July, they said. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose to 1.7302 per dollar early\par on, from Thursday's 1.7334, before offers pulled it back to\par 1.7320.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Analysts said resistance by the region's central banks to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength that threatens export competitiveness would\par continue to cap any significant appreciation.\par     While central banks had been active recently across the\par region, absorbing the recent surge in portfolio inflows, the\par most aggressive policy-driven dollar buying was witnessed in\par Korea. The won hit a four-month high of 1,181.6 per dollar\par before easing to a two-week low of 1,198.5.\par     A dealer in Singapore said Korean state banks on Thursday\par alone soaked up about $400 million on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , surpassing the $325 million worth of stock purchases made\par by foreign investors.\par     Foreign equity investors have been net buyers of Korean\par shares for 16 consecutive session, and have bought a net 998.0\par billion won in shares so far this year.\par     Bhanu Baweja, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at UBS in Singapore, said\par Thursday's move in the won to a four-month high was a test of\par the Bank of Korea's (BOK) sensitivity.\par     "Not surprisingly, it came in to push the won in a big way\par towards the end of the session yesterday," he said. "For the\par near term we therefore continue to see BOK holding firm in the\par battle versus equity market."\par  \par     Authorities in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Thailand\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are also believed to have\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily this week, dealers said.\par     Early on Friday, the baht edged up to 41.56 per dollar,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par above Thursday's one-week low of 41.64. But worries about\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollar buying by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , spotted earlier in the week\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in the 41.40-41.50 area, would keep gains limited, dealers\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Elsewhere, the Philippine peso  rose to a 10-day high\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of 53.26 per dollar, supported by Thursday's report of a lower\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par than expected budget deficit for the five months to March.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers in Manila said the peso's rise would face\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par resistance around the 53.20 level, where dollar bids by\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par corporates and importers were expected.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Indonesian rupiah  rose to 8,230 per dollar, off\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thursday's one-week low around 8,250, on a lack of corporate\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par demand for U.S. dollars. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR AT 0226 GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Change on the day\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move\par  Japan yen     118.01       118.60         +0.50\par  Sing dlr      1.7317       1.7317         +0.00\par  Taiwan dlr    34.585       34.614         +0.08\par  Korean won   1188.50      1198.50         +0.84\par  Baht           41.55        41.60         +0.12\par  Peso           53.20        53.35         +0.28\par  Rupiah       8228.00      8230.00         +0.02\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     118.01       118.74         +0.62\par  Sing dlr      1.7317       1.7341         +0.14\par  Taiwan dlr    34.585       34.753         +0.49\par  Korean won   1188.50      1185.80         -0.23\par  Baht           41.55        43.08         +3.68\par  Peso           53.20        53.38         +0.34\par  Rupiah       8228.00      8940.00         +8.65.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030620dz6k002s2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
